'''Hafnon''' is a [[hafnium]] [[nesosilicate]] [[mineral]] with the idealized chemical formula {{chem2|HfSiO4}}.<ref name="Mindat" /> It is the mineral form of [[Hafnium(IV) silicate|hafnium silicate]] and one of the few known minerals with essential hafnium.

==Chemistry==
Hafnon forms a [[solid solution|solid-solution]] series with its [[zirconium]] counterpart, [[zircon]] ({{chem2|ZrSiO4}}). Several other variants exist, with compositions such as {{chem2|(Hf,Zr,Th,U,Y)SiO4}}.<ref>http://www.minerals.net/mineral/silicate/neso/zircon/hafnon.htm Minerals.net</ref> In nature, part of [[zirconium]] is replaced by the chemically similar hafnium, so natural zircon is never pure {{chem2|ZrSiO4}}. However, a zircon with 100% hafnium substitution can be synthesized and is known as hafnon.

Hafnon occurs as transparent red to red-orange [[tetragonal]] crystals, with a [[Mohs scale|Moh's]] hardness of 7.5.<ref name="Mindat">{{Cite web |title=Hafnon |url=https://www.mindat.org/min-1792.html |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=www.mindat.org |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Hafnon Mineral Data|url=https://webmineral.com/data/Hafnon.shtml|access-date=2021-01-31|website=webmineral.com}}</ref>

Hafnon occurs naturally in [[tantalum]]-bearing [[granite]] [[pegmatite]]s in [[Zambezia Province|Zambezia]] (Mozambique), and in weathered [[pegmatite]]s at Mount Holland, [[Western Australia]].<ref>http://www.handbookofmineralogy.org/pdfs/Hafnon.PDF {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211020021909/http://www.handbookofmineralogy.org/pdfs/Hafnon.PDF |date=2021-10-20 }} Handbook of Mineralogy</ref> It has also been reported at locations in [[Ontario]], [[Quebec]], and [[Manitoba]], Canada; [[North Carolina]], United States, and [[Zimbabwe]].<ref name=Mindat/>

